  "output": "## Findings\n\nThis is an unusually disciplined codebase for the categories you asked about — most of the classic anti-patterns are already designed around. Here's what's actually true, not a generic checklist.\n\n### Important caveat: the engine has no logic to analyze yet\n\nEvery function in `engine/pipeline/*.py`, `engine/agent/graph.py`, `engine/db.py`, `engine/main.py`, `engine/connectors/{warehouse,salesforce,synthetic}.py`, and `engine/eval/*.py` is `raise NotImplementedError(...)` — 16 of 17 Python modules, per AGENTS.md's Phase 1–3 build order. There is no blocking, matching, detection, or resolution code to check for O(n²) comparisons, per-row DB writes, or missing prompt caching, because it doesn't exist yet. I'm not going to invent bugs in stub code. Flagging two things to watch for **when you build these**, since AGENTS.md already commits to them and it's easy to violate accidentally:\n\n- `pipeline/blocking.py`: the docstring says O(n·k), never O(n²) — when you implement `block()`, make sure the embedding/FAISS path only runs on entities that survive exact-key blocking, not the full cross product.\n- `db.py` writes (`write_entities`, `write_matches`, `write_conflicts`): these will be called with thousands of rows per run. Use `psycopg`'s `executemany`/`COPY`, not a loop of single-row `INSERT`s — a naive per-entity insert loop is the actual N+1 risk in this project, just not written yet.\n- `agent/graph.py` + `agent/prompts.py`: AGENTS.md mandates prompt caching on the ruleset block (`cache_control` on that content block in the Anthropic call) since it's identical across every conflict in a run. Easy to forget once `propose()` is actually implemented — it's the single largest cost lever per your own spec.\n\n### N+1 query patterns — none found in `web/lib/server/`\n\nThe query layer already does what a review would normally ask for. `getQueuePage` (`web/lib/server/conflicts.ts:172`) batches rows + counts + total via `Promise.all`, then does exactly **one** grouped query for child-counts across the whole page (`countChildrenByParent`, `web/lib/server/entities.ts:28`) instead of one query per row — the code comments explicitly call this out as the design intent. Same pattern in `export.ts` and `audit.ts`.\n\nOne real, minor opportunity: `getQueuePage` still costs 4 round trips per page load (rows, byStatus, bySeverity, total — `conflicts.ts:172-282`). `byStatus`/`bySeverity`/`total` are all aggregates over the same filtered set and could collapse into one query using conditional aggregation:\n\n```ts\n// instead of two grouped queries + a bare count, one query with FILTER:\nconst [row] = await getDb()\n  .select({\n    total: count(),\n    escalated: sql<number>`count(*) filter (where ${resolutions.status} = 'escalated')`,\n    autoApplied: sql<number>`count(*) filter (where ${resolutions.status} = 'auto_applied')`,\n    sev4: sql<number>`count(*) filter (where ${conflicts.severity} = 4)`,\n    // ...sev3, sev2, sev1\n  })\n  .from(conflicts)\n  .leftJoin(resolutions, eq(resolutions.conflictId, conflicts.id))\n  .where(and(eq(conflicts.runId, runId), ...filters(query, \"status\")));\n```\nThis is a nice-to-have, not a bug — the current version is already correct and batched.\n\n### Unnecessary React re-renders — one real finding, in the core interaction loop\n\n`ConflictQueue` (`web/components/conflicts/conflict-queue.tsx`) holds `selectedIndex`, `pendingId`, `decided`, `overrideConflictId` as local state, and re-maps the full `rows` array (up to `QUEUE_PAGE_SIZE = 100`) on every change. None of the row components — `ConflictDiffRow`, the row `<button>` in `ConflictDenseTable`, `ListRow` in `ConflictFocus` — are wrapped in `React.memo`. So every `j`/`k` keypress re-renders and re-diffs up to 100 row subtrees (each running `diffSegments` character-diffing, `ConflictSides`, `ReviewActions`) just to move a selection outline by one row.\n\nIt's worse than a missing `memo`, though: even adding one wouldn't help today, because `submitFor(row.conflictId)` (conflict-queue.tsx:180) and the inline `onOverrideOpenChange={(open) => setOverrideOpen(row.conflictId, open)}` closures are recreated fresh per row on every render — new prop identity every time defeats memoization.\n\nFix — memoize the row and give it a conflict-id-based callback instead of a pre-bound closure:\n\n```tsx\n// conflict-diff-row.tsx\nexport const ConflictDiffRow = React.memo(function ConflictDiffRow({\n  row, submit, pending, decidedStatus, selected, overrideOpen,\n  onOverrideOpenChange, onSelect, staggerIndex,\n}: Props) {\n  /* unchanged body */\n});\n\n// conflict-queue.tsx — submit takes the id itself, so the callback is stable\nconst submit_ = useCallback(\n  (conflictId: string, action: ReviewAction) => submit(conflictId, action),\n  [submit],\n);\n\n{rows.map((row, index) => (\n  <ConflictDiffRow\n    key={row.conflictId}\n    row={row}\n    selected={index === clamped}\n    onSelect={() => setSelectedIndex(index)}\n    submit={submit_}                 // stable across renders\n    conflictId={row.conflictId}      // row invokes submit(conflictId, action) itself\n    pending={pendingId === row.conflictId}\n    decidedStatus={decided[row.conflictId]?.status ?? null}\n    overrideOpen={overrideConflictId === row.conflictId}\n    onOverrideOpenChange={setOverrideOpen}  // already stable, already takes conflictId\n    staggerIndex={staggerIndex(index)}\n  />\n))}\n```\nWith this, moving the selection only re-renders the previously-selected and newly-selected rows (2 of up to 100), which is what a reviewer working a 400-conflict queue (your own copy) actually needs.\n\n### Redundant computation — `RulesetEditor`\n\n`web/components/rules/ruleset-editor.tsx:166-179`:\n```tsx\n{ordered.map((rule) => {\n  const index = rules.indexOf(rule);        // O(n) per row\n  return (\n    <RuleRow\n      position={ordered.indexOf(rule) + 1}  // O(n) per row, again\n      ...\n```\nTwo `indexOf` calls inside a `.map` is O(n²). Rule counts are small today, so this isn't costing anything real yet, but it's a one-line fix and the pattern is worth not carrying forward:\n```tsx\nconst indexById = new Map(rules.map((r, i) => [r.id, i]));\n{ordered.map((rule, position) => (\n  <RuleRow\n    key={rule.id}\n    position={position + 1}\n    onChange={(patch) => update(indexById.get(rule.id)!, patch)}\n    ...\n```\n\n### Caching opportunities\n\n- The one AGENTS.md actually calls out — prompt caching on the ruleset block in `engine/agent/graph.py` — doesn't exist yet because the graph isn't implemented (see caveat above). This is the highest-leverage caching opportunity in the whole project once you get to Phase 3; don't lose it in the first draft of `propose()`.\n- The query-count consolidation above (4 round trips → 1–2) is the only live DB-caching-adjacent opportunity; there's no in-memory/HTTP caching layer to add on top given the deliberate \"everything is a Server Component reading Postgres directly\" design in AGENTS.md, and adding one would fight that design rather than improve it.\n\n### Memory leaks — none found\n\nChecked the usual suspects: `RunLive`'s `setInterval` (`Elapsed`, run-live.tsx:106) and the `ConflictQueue` `keydown` listener (conflict-queue.tsx:130) both clean up correctly in their `useEffect` return. `QueryProvider` scopes a fresh `QueryClient` per mount via `useState`, not a module singleton. `lib/db/index.ts` deliberately caches the `postgres()` connection on `globalThis` specifically to avoid leaking a new pool on every Next.js HMR reload. Nothing leaked.\n\n---\n\n**Bottom line:** the web layer is already doing the right thing on queries; the one concrete, worth-fixing issue is the conflict-queue re-render cost, since that's the screen your own UI copy says has to handle 400 rows keyboard-only. The engine has nothing to optimize yet — it has nothing implemented yet.\n",
